    "body": "## Root cause\n\n`daily_agenda` built the grouping key from the raw UTC instant (`slot.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\")`) while deriving the displayed time from the local conversion (`to_local(slot, tz_name)`). Two sources of truth for the same slot: the hour was always right, the day was wrong whenever the UTC date and the user's local date disagree. That is why `2026-03-16 02:00Z` in `America/Mexico_City` rendered as `{\"2026-03-16\": [\"20:00\"]}` — the correct 20:00, filed under the wrong calendar cell.\n\n## What changed and why\n\n`scheduler/booking.py` — convert each slot to the user's zone **once** and derive both the day key and the time from that single conversion. This removes the second source of truth rather than compensating for it, so there is no offset arithmetic to get wrong and no `try/except` hiding the symptom. The offset stays resolved per instant by `zoneinfo`, so DST transitions keep working.\n\nThe docstring now states the contract that was missing: slots must be timezone-aware, and grouping by the UTC date shifts a slot to the *next* cell west of UTC but the *previous* one east of it.\n\n`tests/test_booking.py` — `daily_agenda` had zero coverage, which is why this shipped. Added the reported repro, two UTC days collapsing into one local day, the reverse direction (one UTC day splitting into two local days), the east-of-UTC direction (Tokyo), a shift that rolls day/month/year at once, two distinct day keys, a DST spring-forward case, a DST fall-back case, sort order, and the empty agenda.\n\n## How it was verified\n\n- Full suite green: `python3 -m unittest discover -v` → **13 tests, OK**.\n- **Fail-without-fix proven, not assumed:** restoring `scheduler/booking.py` from the base commit makes **6 of the 13 tests fail**; restoring the fix returns the suite to green.\n- Tests are timezone-hermetic — the suite passes under `TZ=UTC`, `Pacific/Kiritimati`, `America/Mexico_City` and `Asia/Tokyo`.\n- The two DST tests earn their place against plausible *wrong* fixes: a \"compute the offset once and shift\" implementation passes the reported Mexico City case but returns `02:00` for New York on 2026-03-08 — a wall-clock time that does not exist that day — and an \"offset subtraction\" fix is caught by the Tokyo case.\n\nNote: `America/Mexico_City` has no DST in 2026 (Mexico abolished it in 2022, fixed at UTC−6), so the reported zone cannot test per-instant offset resolution. `America/New_York` is used for that.\n\n## Review\n\nA second adversarial review pass ran against this branch (three layers: Blind Hunter, Edge Case Hunter, Acceptance Auditor). It found two coverage gaps, which are fixed in `8fec774`, and **two questions that need a human ruling** — both published as review comments on this PR. Neither blocks the root-cause fix; both are about behaviour this change deliberately did not settle.\n\nCloses #1\n\n---\n\n🤖 This PR was opened by the automated harness and is awaiting human review.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
